# TLS server, CHUNKING reception with TLS shutdown mid-chunk
gnutls
#
# A TLS session that goes away while a BDAT chunk is still being read leaves the
# server holding readers, and a transfer buffer, belonging to a session that has
# ceased to exist.  Reading has to stop there.  Going on would either reach into
# the released buffer or take the rest of the chunk from the socket in clear, on
# the connection the peer negotiated encryption for.
#
# Chunk arithmetic - 88 declared, 20 sent, so 68 bytes are still outstanding when
# the session goes.  The ">>> " prefix suppresses the trailing CRLF the client
# would otherwise add, so the payload is exactly the 20 characters after it.  The
# chunk has to be short: what was declared is then still incomplete when the
# session ends, and a reader which treated what arrived as the whole of the
# message would accept a message the peer never finished sending.
#
# The reply is matched in full rather than as just "421" because the receive
# timeout answers with a 421 of its own, and a loose match would be satisfied by
# the very behaviour this test exists to reject - a server that keeps reading the
# plain socket has nothing to read there, and says so when the timeout expires.
#
# Nothing is offered in clear after the shutdown, so the server has nothing
# unconsumed to reset over and its close is clean.  That is what lets the end of
# file be asserted here, and asserting it is half the point: a server which
# answered 421 and then left the socket usable would pass on the reply alone.
#
# Note for running this by hand: the daemon under test has to be the GnuTLS
# build.  Hand that binary to runtest and leave --tls off:
#
#     cd test && ./runtest <gnutls-binary> --continue --test 2038
#
# That is the case in which the binary runtest was given is used at both ends, as
# $exim_client and as $exim_server alike.  Given --tls, runtest works in its
# cross-library mode instead: it picks the harness client program, and it also
# pairs the two library-specific builds against each other, so "--tls gnutls"
# leaves the OpenSSL build answering as $exim_server.  That build never frees the
# transfer buffer this test is about, and it answers this dialogue in a way no
# client can read: its own read path records an error without unregistering the
# session, so the 421 below is written through the session the peer has already
# dropped and the peer reaches end of file without seeing it.  Thirteen
# pre-existing tests in this directory fail in that mode for the same kind of
# reason - 2000, 2001, 2002, 2012, 2014, 2024, 2025, 2027, 2029, 2031, 2033,
# 2034 and 2090 - and the category's REQUIRES cannot express a condition on
# $exim_server, only on the binary handed to runtest.  Test numbers spanning more
# than one script directory have to be asked for as a range, or one batch per
# directory.
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 88 LAST
>>> To: Susan@random.com
stoptls
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
???*
****
millisleep 500
killdaemon
#
no_msglog_check
